The strict pairing of two elements is the element of the smash whose neighbourhoods are the product of the two masters together with products of a proper neighbourhood of the first element and a proper neighbourhood of the second. Such a product is a smash neighbourhood. The master belongs by the master clause. Intersecting with the master absorbs that factor, because each neighbourhood lies inside its master, and the intersection of two proper products is the product of the intersections, which remain proper. A larger neighbourhood is either the master or a proper product, and in the proper case the factor inclusions pass to the two elements.
